Patric Mueller wrote:
> I think there are already enough possibilities to spend ones gold.
> You can buy stuff in shops, ask the Oracle for direction or donate to
> priests.

I disagree -- there's far too much gold in the game relative its uses.
By the end, players almost always have tens of thousands of gold pieces
lying around without purpose, which either gets toted up to the high
altar for what amounts to an extremely minor score bonus, or left
behind in the dungeon.

Part of money's uselessness is Nethack's philosophy that any NPC can be
attacked and killed.  What's to stop a player from killing someone cash
has been given to and getting it back?  If a lawful character does this
to a Shopkeeper, it's not even a huge alignment penalty.  Doing this
with a priest will at least cause the player to lose all protection,
which at least shows the devteam is looking at the implications of NPC
murder in the game's for-pay services.

Nethack+'s solution, when it offered shopkeeper services, was to make
shopkeepers badasses that even high-level characters had to think twice
before crossing.  That was a feature of Nethack+ that didn't make it
into vanilla, although we have gotten a smattering of other features
(most notably weapon skills).

What I would do is:
1. Severely increase the player's score bonus for gold, maybe by a
factor of ten.  Considerations would have to be made for characters who
start with extra gold, and maybe it should only be a x5 bonus if the
player doesn't ascend.
2. Make shopkeepers tougher.  Even if they don't provide services.
3. Find some non-economic uses for gold.  Gold is rustproof, maybe a
large quantity of gold pieces can be used to apply plating to a weapon?
 What can real-life gold be used for that could be brought into the
game to serve as an unrecoverable sink for lots of gold pieces?
4. Find services that can be sold to the player that results in gold
being unrecoverable.  Maybe vending machines?  Maybe a monster NPC that
eats gold given to him?

Just brainstorming here.

